      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah and this is intentional to skip access to the pkvm.text section. This prevents decoding the size of the static branch instruction, which would cause a page fault after deprivileging because pkvm.text is already unmapped at that point.\n\nWhat I actually tried at the beginning was a bit cleaner approach and modify `kernel/jump_label.c`\u0027s   `__jump_label_text_reserved()` with\n```\n        iter \u003d iter_start;\n        while (iter \u003c iter_stop) {\n-               if (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter)) {\n+               if (!is_pkvm_text((void *)jump_entry_code(iter)) \u0026\u0026\n+                   (init || !jump_entry_is_init(iter))) {\n```\n\nand it will work as well but then we will end-up with modifying generic non-x86 code, which I wanted to avoid.\n\nSo modifying `arch_jump_entry_size` this way (to return 0 for pkvm.text) will prevent `jump_label_text_reserved` from decoding unmapped region. And I think it should be safe since it has impact only on `addr_conflict()`, which is only used for `__jump_label_text_reserved` (which itself is used only for kprobes case anyway, and probing .pkvm.text itself is blocked by the kprobe blacklist in the next patch).\n\nBTW it is also kind of similar (though implicit) to how jump_label_text_reserved() already treats .init.text entries, but again in a way that modifies only x86 code.",
